<h1>Exercise Name:Side Crunches (also called Lying Side Crunches or Oblique Crunches).</h1>
<b><u><h4>Video Overview:</h4></u></b> The 3D anatomical animation shows a model lying on their side on an exercise mat with knees<br><br> stacked and slightly bent. One hand is placed behind the head while the torso curls laterally upward, <br><br>activating the side abdominal muscles (obliques), which are highlighted in red and yellow to demonstrate target muscular engagement.
</div>
</div>
<pre>

 <h1>Step-by-Step How to Perform</h1>

<b><u><h4>1.Starting Position:</h4></u></b> Lie on your side on a mat with your knees stacked and bent at roughly a 90-degree angle. Place your top hand lightly behind your head (or behind your ear) and rest your bottom arm comfortably on the mat or across your chest.
<b><u><h4>2.Engage the Obliques: </h4></u></b>Focus your attention on your side waist (obliques). Keep your hips static throughout the movement.
<b><u><h4>3.Crunch Upward:</h4></u></b> Exhale as you flex your torso sideways, bringing your top elbow toward your top hip. Lift your upper chest off the floor using your side abs.
<b><u><h4>4.Squeeze at the Top: </h4></u></b>Pause briefly at the top of the motion for a second to contract your oblique muscles fully.
<b><u><h4>5.Lower with Control:</h4></u></b>Inhale as you slowly lower your torso back down to the starting position without resting completely at the bottom.
<b><u><h4>6.Switch Sides:</h4></u></b> Complete all reps on one side before flipping over to perform the same number on the other side.



<h1> Key Benefits</h1>

<b>◉Targeted Oblique Development:</b> Specifically strengthens the internal and external obliques, helping build waist stability and rotational power.
<b>◉Core Symmetry & Balance:</b> Unilateral (one-sided) movements correct strength imbalances between the right and left sides of your abdominal wall.
<b>◉Tapered Waistline:</b> Tones and tightens the sides of the torso, complementing standard front-facing abdominal exercises.
<b>◉Spine Mobility:</b> Enhances lateral flexion (side-bending movement) of the spine safely without heavy loads.



<h1>Reps, Sets & Recommendations</h1>

Perform the listed reps oneach side per set.

| Level | Reps Per Side | Sets | Rest Period |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
|Beginner | 10 – 12 reps | 3 Sets | 45 – 60 seconds |
|Intermediate | 12 – 15 reps | 3 – 4 Sets | 45 seconds |
|Advanced | 15 – 20 reps | 4 Sets | 30 – 45 seconds |

Tip: Avoid pulling on your neck or head with your hands to force the lift. Drive the movement entirely by crunching your side ribs toward your hip bone.
